[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A rendered view of the canon is a regenerable deliverable, not an in-session expansion

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-VIEWS-ARE-REGENERABLE
TITLE: A rendered view of the canon is a regenerable deliverable, not an in-session expansion
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: A reader of the canon exists that is neither a model nor a person reading a rendered view.
STATEMENT: >>>
A person reads the canon through rendered views: pages generated from the
export by scripts that are themselves registered in the canon with File
relations, over narratives that are themselves nodes. A view is regenerated
whenever the canon changes; it is never hand-edited and never the source. Its
wireline -- a versioned JSON payload -- is the contract between the canon and
any reader of it, so the viewer's FORM stays open (DEC-VIEWER-FORM-UNDECIDED):
a terminal client, a web page and a published artifact are all readers of the
same payload.

DEC-HITL-IS-THE-VIEWER said human reading is served by in-session expansion by
a model, not by a rendered artifact. That held until the semantic-layer render
of 2026-08-29 showed the operator reads rendered structure and cannot read the
same content as prose, and they asked for the view to be permanent and
iterable. This decision supersedes it.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Three text presentations of one sheet failed in a single session before a
rendered page succeeded (SLICE-SEMANTIC-LAYER-RENDER). A view that only exists
while a session is open cannot be iterated on, reviewed, or combed for
decisions; one generated from the canon can.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Ruled 2026-08-30 as a first-pass decision under the operator's delegation;
overrule by superseding. Their words: "what would it take to build this as a
permanent view to iterate on" and "ideally I could flip the view to the
whiteboard design and enhance it that way". The view system itself is the
whiteboard-view plan.
<<<
